\subsection{Finite-product search gives a one-sided procedure}
Let $\mathcal A$ be a finite nonempty set of rational square matrices of the same size. For $k\ge1$, define $a_k$ to be the largest induced row-sum norm of a product of $k$ members of $\mathcal A$, and let $\rho=\limsup_{k\to\infty}a_k^{1/k}$. This is the usual joint spectral radius norm formulation. We test exact enumeration as a route to deciding strict stability.
\begin{proposition}
Enumerating products and stopping when $a_k<1$ is a correct procedure that halts on every instance with $\rho<1$. It does not supply a termination guarantee when $\rho\ge1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The induced norm is submultiplicative, so $a_{u+v}\le a_ua_v$, with $a_0=1$. If $a_k<1$, write $N=qk+r$ with $0\le r<k$. Then
\[
a_N\le a_k^q\max_{0\le j<k}a_j.
\]
If $a_k=0$, all sufficiently long products vanish and $\rho=0$. Otherwise, taking $N$th roots and the limit superior gives $\rho\le a_k^{1/k}<1$. Conversely, if $\rho<1$, choose $c$ strictly between $\rho$ and 1. The definition of limit superior implies $a_k^{1/k}<c$ for all sufficiently large $k$, so enumeration eventually finds $a_k<1$. At each fixed $k$ there are finitely many products and their rational entries and row-sum norms can be computed and compared exactly. On a nonstable input no such certificate can occur, but this enumeration does not produce a finite negative certificate.
\end{proof}
Checking the eigenvalues of individual matrices is insufficient. Consider
\[
A=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Both are nilpotent, but $AB=\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$. All products have row-sum norm at most 1, while arbitrarily long alternating products have norm 1, so their joint spectral radius is exactly 1. Multiplying both matrices by $1/2$ instead gives $a_k=2^{-k}$ and joint spectral radius $1/2$.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
All products through length eight were enumerated with exact rational arithmetic in these two examples. The unscaled case never produces the strict certificate; the scaled case does so at length one. The attempted route therefore proves only semidecision of strict stability. It neither decides the boundary nor proves finite-product attainment of the joint spectral radius. The 2007 primary paper was read for its historical finiteness/decidability connection; its then-current status statements are not asserted to describe the complete 2026 literature. No new decision theorem or finiteness result is claimed.

